  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.454,
            0.071,
            0.813,
            0.089
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Math of Space: Surface Area and Volume"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.841,
            0.071,
            0.881,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "125"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.111,
            0.883,
            0.229
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Do you remember cube numbers? Cube numbers are the numbers in the sequence 1, 8, 27, 64, … . These numbers represent the number of unit cubes that can fit inside cubes of side lengths 1, 2, 3, 4, … respectively — in other words, they represent the volumes of these cubes. We know that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "1^{3} = 1, 2^{3} = 8, 3^{3} = 27, 4^{3} = 64,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " …; and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\sqrt[3]{1} = 1, \\sqrt[3]{8} = 2, \\sqrt[3]{27} = 3, \\sqrt[3]{64} = 4,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and so on."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.24,
            0.882,
            0.336
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 1:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Two solid objects are made from the same material: Cube A has side 6 cm and Cuboid B has dimensions 9 cm × 6 cm × 4 cm. Compare their volumes and surface areas and determine which object has a greater surface area. How is this useful in a real-life situation?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.342,
            0.447,
            0.362
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "For Cube A, side (a) = 6 cm."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.367,
            0.881,
            0.405
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Hence its volume is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a^{3} = (6\\  cm )^{3} = 216\\  cm ^{3}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and surface area (SA) is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "6a^{2}=6\\times(6\\ cm)^{2}=216\\ cm^{2}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.411,
            0.862,
            0.431
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "For Cuboid B, length (l) = 9 cm; width (w) = 6 cm; height (h) = 4 cm."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.435,
            0.68,
            0.455
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Hence its volume is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "l \\times w \\times h = 9 \\times 6 \\times 4 = 216\\  cm ^3"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.46,
            0.88,
            0.499
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "and surface area (SA) is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2(lw \\mp wh \\mp lh) = 2[(9 \\times 6) \\div (6 \\times 4) \\div (4 \\times 9)]"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "= 2 2 8 \\mathrm { c m ^ { 2 } } ."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.504,
            0.881,
            0.545
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Thus, the two objects have the same volume but different surface areas. The cuboid has greater surface area than the cube."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.548,
            0.881,
            0.589
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Conclusion:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " For the same storage capacity: a cube requires less covering material; a cuboid exposes more area to its surroundings."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.593,
            0.882,
            0.634
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This concept is important in packaging, heat transfer and engineering."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.365,
            0.659,
            0.63,
            0.687
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Exercise Set 14.1"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.707,
            0.851,
            0.728
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. The volume of a cube is 64 cm"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3",
              "styles": [
                "superscript"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". What is its total surface area?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.732,
            0.881,
            0.772
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. How many small cubes with side 20 cm can be packed tight in a cubical box with side 2 m?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.776,
            0.882,
            0.835
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. The dimensions of a godown are 40 m × 25 m × 10 m. If it is filled with cuboidal boxes, each of dimensions 2 m × 1.25 m × 1 m, then find the number of boxes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.839,
            0.881,
            0.88
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Two cubes each of volume 125 cm"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3",
              "styles": [
                "superscript"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " are joined end to end. Find the surface area of the resulting cuboid."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
